(window.THART=window.THART||{})[134]={"h": "<section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c1\" data-t=\"Operating note\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">1</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter one</div><h2>Operating note</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">The case on one page.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s1-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">1.1</span><h3>The case at a glance</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Mastoidectomy</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"></th><th scope=\"col\">Summary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Indications</th><td>Cholesteatoma of the attic or mastoid; chronic suppurative mastoiditis without cholesteatoma; acute coalescent mastoiditis, with or without temporal bone or intracranial complications; access for cochlear implantation, facial nerve surgery (decompression, tumour, grafting), endolymphatic sac surgery, labyrinthectomy, temporal bone resection and approaches to the posterior fossa and petrous apex</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Canal wall up (intact)</th><td>Drainage of acute mastoiditis; cholesteatoma that can be fully removed or will have a planned second look; implant and facial nerve surgery</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Canal wall down</th><td>Small, sclerotic mastoid with limited exposure; low tegmen or forward sigmoid sinus; disease that cannot be fully removed and must be exteriorised; labyrinthine fistula; matrix on an eroded footplate or a widely dehiscent facial nerve; an only hearing ear; unreliable follow-up; revision surgery with higher risk</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Contraindications</th><td>Unfit for general anaesthesia; poor wound healing; cholesteatoma extending beyond the temporal bone (mastoidectomy alone is inadequate). An auto-atticotomy or auto-mastoidectomy cavity may be managed by cleaning in clinic</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Anaesthesia</th><td>General, <b>without muscle relaxation</b> after induction, whether or not the facial nerve is monitored; controlled hypotension helps</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Position</th><td>Supine, head turned away, neck neutral or slightly flexed; strapped to the table at several points because the table will be rotated; facial nerve electrodes placed and tested</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Antibiotics</th><td>First-generation cephalosporin; clindamycin or vancomycin in penicillin allergy</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Tray</th><td>Tympanoplasty and mastoidectomy set, otological drill with cutting and diamond burs in a full range of sizes, suction irrigation, operating microscope (225 to 300 mm lens), otoendoscopes for hidden areas, facial nerve monitor, 1:1000 adrenaline pledgets (counted), Gelfoam, Surgicel and bone wax</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Imaging</th><td>Axial and coronal CT of the temporal bones available in theatre</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s1-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">1.2</span><h3>Consent</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-consent\"><h5>What to tell the patient</h5><ul><li><b>Why:</b> to remove infection or skin trapped in the ear (cholesteatoma) and make the ear safe and dry. Hearing is a secondary aim and may not improve; it may be worse.</li><li><b>Further surgery:</b> a planned second look after 9 to 12 months, a staged hearing reconstruction, or revision for recurrence.</li><li><b>A mastoid cavity</b> if the canal wall is taken down: a wider ear opening, lifelong cleaning in clinic, care with water, and sometimes dizziness with cold air or water.</li><li><b>Hearing loss</b>, including a small risk of total loss in that ear, and <b>tinnitus</b>.</li><li><b>Dizziness</b>, usually temporary.</li><li><b>Facial weakness:</b> under 1% in a first operation, about 4% to 10% in revisions; usually temporary when it occurs.</li><li><b>Altered taste</b> on that side of the tongue, often recovering over months.</li><li><b>Leak of brain fluid, meningitis</b> or bleeding from the large vein behind the ear: rare.</li><li><b>Wound problems</b>, numbness of the ear, and a change in the position of the pinna after meatoplasty.</li><li>A <b>hearing aid</b> may still be needed.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s1-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">1.3</span><h3>Before you start</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Ask about the duration and pattern of discharge, hearing loss, otalgia and dizziness; trauma, water exposure and barotrauma; response to treatment; and red flags: rapid progression, headache or altered mental state (intracranial complication), facial weakness and vertigo. Take the otological past: childhood infections and surgery, previous mastoid surgery or implants, noise, ototoxic drugs, radiotherapy and hearing aid use. <b>The other ear</b> decides a great deal: bilateral cholesteatoma, a contralateral perforation or hearing loss change how conservative you must be. Assess the patient's ability to attend for cavity care and second-stage surgery. Examine both ears under the microscope after cleaning, and again after treating infection: an attic retraction pocket, keratin, a polyp, erosion of the scutum, the state of the pars tensa and a narrow canal that will need canalplasty. Record Rinne and Weber. Examine the nasopharynx, the cranial nerves and the fundi. A postauricular swelling, an acute facial palsy or papilloedema needs urgent action. Perform a fistula test when vertigo is reported. Obtain bilateral audiometry (auditory brainstem responses in a child who cannot be tested), but never delay emergency surgery for it. <b>CT</b> is the anatomical map: on axial cuts, the pneumatisation and the position of the sigmoid sinus; on coronal cuts, the level of the tegmen; and erosion of the lateral semicircular canal, tegmen, facial canal and ossicles. CT cannot reliably show the stapes or tell cholesteatoma from fluid or mucosa unless bone is eroded. <b>MRI</b> shows encephalocele (coronal T2), intracranial complications, and, on non-echo-planar <b>diffusion-weighted imaging</b>, cholesteatoma itself. Control infection with topical and systemic antibiotics and aural toilet before elective surgery.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c2\" data-t=\"Anatomy you must see\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">2</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter two</div><h2>Anatomy you must see</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">Landmarks in the order you meet them.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s2-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">2.1</span><h3>On the surface of the bone</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>The linea temporalis (supramastoid crest):</b> the ridge running back from the root of the zygoma at the lower edge of the temporalis; it marks roughly the level of the <b>middle fossa dura</b>.</li><li><b>The spine of Henle:</b> the small spur at the posterosuperior margin of the bony meatus.</li><li><b>MacEwen's (suprameatal) triangle,</b> with the <b>cribriform area</b> of small vessel holes: bounded by the linea temporalis above, the posterosuperior meatal wall in front and a tangent to the back of the canal behind. The <b>antrum</b> lies deep to it, about 12 to 15 mm in the adult and only a few millimetres in an infant.</li><li><b>The mastoid tip,</b> with the digastric groove on its inner side.</li></ul></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s2-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">2.2</span><h3>In the cavity</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>The tegmen mastoideum,</b> the plate of bone under the middle fossa dura: it shows as a pink blush through thin bone, sometimes with small veins.</li><li><b>The sigmoid sinus,</b> behind and medial: a blue blush through its bony plate. Between them is the <b>sinodural angle</b>.</li><li><b>Koerner's septum</b> (the petrosquamous lamina), a plate of bone that can be mistaken for the medial wall of the antrum; the true antrum is deep to it.</li><li><b>The lateral (horizontal) semicircular canal:</b> a smooth, dense, ivory-coloured dome in the floor of the antrum. It is the most reliable landmark in the mastoid.</li><li><b>The short process of the incus</b> in the <b>fossa incudis</b>, seen in front of the canal through the aditus.</li><li><b>The digastric ridge</b> at the tip: its front end leads to the stylomastoid foramen.</li></ul></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s2-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">2.3</span><h3>The facial nerve</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The <b>tympanic segment</b> runs back above the oval window, below the lateral canal. The <b>second genu</b> lies just below and medial to the dome of the lateral canal, at the posterior edge of the oval window and medial to the short process of the incus, which points to it; this is the segment injured most often. The <b>mastoid (vertical) segment</b> descends from there to the stylomastoid foramen at the front of the digastric ridge, lying deeper above and more lateral as it descends. The <b>chorda tympani</b> leaves it a few millimetres above the foramen and runs up and forward to the annulus. The <b>facial recess</b> is the triangle between the facial nerve medially, the chorda laterally and the incus buttress above.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c3\" data-t=\"The operation, stage by stage\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">3</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter three</div><h2>The operation, stage by stage</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">Intact canal wall (cortical) mastoidectomy.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.1</span><h3>Set up, inject and incise</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Prepare the field, check the monitor, and examine and clean the ear under the microscope to confirm the findings. If a tympanomeatal flap will be raised, inject lidocaine with adrenaline in four quadrants at the bony-cartilaginous junction (the medial limit of the hair-bearing skin), bevel to the bone and subperiosteal, until the skin blanches down to the drum; then make canal incisions at 12 and 6 o'clock joined by a vertical cut halfway between the annulus and the junction, forming a laterally based vascular strip and a medially based tympanomeatal flap. Make the postauricular incision a few millimetres behind the crease, from above the root of the helix to the tip, down to the loose areolar tissue over the temporalis fascia. In children, keep the lower limb further back and off the tip.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f1\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/134_1\" data-set=\"thm134\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 1\"><img src=\"fig2/m/134_1.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 1</b>The H-shaped canal incisions: a laterally based vascular strip and a medially based tympanomeatal flap.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 134.1</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>Canal skin blanched to the drum, and the glistening temporalis fascia with loose areolar tissue over it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>The child&#x27;s facial nerve</h5><p>The mastoid tip is undeveloped in small children and the nerve leaves the skull superficially. Curve the incision back and stop short of the tip.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.2</span><h3>Expose the cortex and the landmarks</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Incise the periosteum along the linea temporalis with a vertical limb to the tip (a \"T\" or \"7\"), elevate it forward to expose the posterior bony canal, lift the vascular strip, and retract the pinna forward with a tape or Penrose passed through the canal. Harvest temporalis fascia now and set it to dry. Identify the linea temporalis, the spine of Henle and the cribriform area.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The whole mastoid cortex from the root of the zygoma to the tip, the posterior canal wall, and MacEwen's triangle with its small pits.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-senior\"><h5>Senior&#x27;s pearl</h5><p>Exposure decides the operation. If you cannot see the zygomatic root and the tip, you will drill a deep narrow hole, which is how facial nerves are injured.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.3</span><h3>Make the first cuts and saucerise</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>With the largest cutting bur that fits and continuous suction irrigation, make the first cut along the <b>linea temporalis</b>, the second parallel to the <b>posterior canal wall</b>, and join them behind along the line of the <b>sigmoid sinus</b>, forming a triangle. Remove the cortex and the air cells within it in long strokes parallel to the structure you are approaching, bevelling (saucerising) the edges widely so light and instruments reach the depth.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f2\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/134_2\" data-set=\"thm134\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 2\"><img src=\"fig2/m/134_2.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 2</b>The first bone cuts on the cortex outline the linea temporalis, the posterior canal wall and the sigmoid sinus.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 134.2</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A wide, shallow, kidney-shaped cavity with sloping edges, deepest at its front and top.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>A deep hole</h5><p>Keep <b>the deepest point over the antrum</b>, behind the spine of Henle. Beginners drift down and back, away from the antrum and into the plane of the facial nerve and the sigmoid sinus. If you cannot find the antrum, go forward and up, never down.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.4</span><h3>Define the tegmen and the sinus</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Thin the bone above until the <b>tegmen</b> shows as a pink plate, and behind until the <b>sigmoid sinus</b> shows blue through its plate, leaving a thin shell on each. Open the sinodural angle between them. These two plates are the upper and posterior walls of your cavity and are followed forward to the antrum.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A smooth pink tegmen above, a blue sinus plate behind, and the posterior canal wall thinned in front.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Dura and sinus</h5><p>A cutting bur run across exposed dura or the sinus tears it. Change to a diamond bur as you approach them, and stroke along them, not into them.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-5\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.5</span><h3>Enter the antrum</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Using the tegmen as the roof and the canal wall as the front, remove <b>Koerner's septum</b> and enter the antrum. Confirm it by its size and by what lies in its floor: the dense, pale dome of the <b>lateral semicircular canal</b>.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A large air space or sac of disease, and in its floor the smooth ivory bulge of the lateral canal.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-senior\"><h5>Senior&#x27;s pearl</h5><p>If you think you are in the antrum but cannot see the lateral canal, you are probably still lateral to Koerner's septum. The antrum is deeper than you expect.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-6\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.6</span><h3>Open the aditus and find the incus</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Change to a smaller bur and drill forward toward the root of the zygoma, between the tegmen and the canal wall, keeping the bur turning away from the ossicles, until the <b>short process of the incus</b> appears in the fossa incudis. Flood the cavity: the incus is often first seen as a refraction under the water. Remove remaining cells from the tip, exposing the <b>digastric ridge</b>, and from the sinodural angle, as the disease requires.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f3\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/134_3\" data-set=\"thm134\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 3\"><img src=\"fig2/m/134_3.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 3</b>A completed intact canal wall mastoidectomy: the aditus, the short process of the incus, the lateral semicircular canal and the digastric ridge.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 134.3</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The incus in its fossa, the lateral canal behind and below it, the digastric ridge at the tip, and an intact thin posterior canal wall.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Touching the incus</h5><p>A rotating bur touching the incus transmits its energy to the inner ear and causes high-tone sensorineural loss. Leave a sliver of bone over the short process and remove it with a pick or curette.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-7\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.7</span><h3>Close</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Replace the vascular strip and pack the canal with Gelfoam or Telfa. Close the periosteum (which stops the scar retracting into the cavity), then the deep dermis with 3-0 or 4-0 absorbable sutures and the skin with a fine absorbable suture. Apply a mastoid dressing for 24 hours. Count the pledgets.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c4\" data-t=\"The operation: facial recess (posterior tympanotomy)\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">4</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter four</div><h2>The operation: facial recess (posterior tympanotomy)</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">Into the middle ear with the canal wall intact.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.1</span><h3>Thin the canal wall and set the landmarks</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The recess is bounded by the <b>incus buttress</b> above, the <b>facial nerve</b> medially and behind, and the <b>chorda tympani</b> laterally and in front. Before entering it, have the lateral canal, the fossa incudis and the digastric ridge in view, and thin the posterior canal wall well: a thick wall hides the recess and forces you medially toward the nerve.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The short process of the incus pointing to the recess, and a canal wall thin enough to see instruments through.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.2</span><h3>Find the nerve and open the recess</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>With a small diamond bur and copious irrigation, drill just below the incus buttress in strokes <b>parallel to the course of the facial nerve</b>, never across it. Identify the nerve through a thin covering of bone as a pink-white cord with its fine vessels, and the chorda coming off it laterally. Enter the middle ear at the <b>upper end</b> of the recess, where it is widest.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The facial nerve seen through bone medially, the chorda laterally, and a dark slit between them opening into the middle ear.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Heat and the nerve</h5><p>A diamond bur generates heat quickly; without irrigation it burns the nerve through intact bone. Keep the water running and leave a shell of bone on the nerve.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.3</span><h3>Widen and inspect</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Widen the opening by removing bone medial to the chorda. Through it you see the incudostapedial joint, the stapes and stapedius tendon, the promontory and the round window niche. In an <b>extended facial recess</b>, the chorda is sacrificed and bone removed down to the inferior annulus to expose the hypotympanum and the medial surface of the drum.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f4\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/134_4\" data-set=\"thm134\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 4\"><img src=\"fig2/m/134_4.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 4</b>The middle ear through the completed facial recess: chorda tympani, incudostapedial joint, stapes, promontory and round window niche.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 134.4</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The incudostapedial joint and round window niche, with the sinus tympani hidden medial to the nerve (use an angled endoscope for it).</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-senior\"><h5>Senior&#x27;s pearl</h5><p>Dissect disease off the stapes from back to front, in the direction of the stapedius tendon, which braces the stapes against your pull.</p></div></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c5\" data-t=\"The operation: canal wall down\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">5</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter five</div><h2>The operation: canal wall down</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">The open cavity.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.1</span><h3>Decide and deal with the incus</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Complete the cortical mastoidectomy first. If the incus is diseased or the chain must be interrupted, <b>separate the incudostapedial joint before</b> taking the wall down, so that drilling on the bridge cannot be transmitted to the stapes. If the incus can stay, leave a thin bridge of bone over its short process and remove it with a pick or curette.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A divided incudostapedial joint, or a protected incus under a thin bony bridge.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.2</span><h3>Take down the wall</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Remove the posterior canal wall with a 4 mm cutting bur. Think of it as three parts: the <b>anterior buttress</b> (at the tympanosquamous suture and the lateral attic wall), the <b>posterior buttress</b> (which becomes the facial ridge), and the <b>bridge</b> between them over the aditus. Remove the bridge, then lower both buttresses so the attic roof runs smoothly into the tegmen and the anterior attic is open.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f5\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/134_5\" data-set=\"thm134\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 5\"><img src=\"fig2/m/134_5.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 5</b>A completed canal wall down cavity.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 134.5</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The attic, antrum and mastoid as one cavity with the canal, with no overhang at the anterior attic.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.3</span><h3>Lower the facial ridge</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Thin the bone over the second genu and vertical segment with a diamond bur, in strokes parallel to the nerve, until the ridge is level with the floor of the canal and the nerve is covered by only a thin plate. A <b>high facial ridge</b> leaves a sump behind it that collects debris and discharges for life.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A low, smooth ridge with the nerve's course visible through bone, the lateral canal dome above it, and a round rather than kidney-shaped cavity.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>The second genu</h5><p>This is where the nerve is injured: just below the lateral canal, at the level of the fossa incudis. Above the canal you are safe; at and below it, use the diamond bur and the monitor.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.4</span><h3>Saucerise and remove the disease</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Bevel the upper and posterior edges of the cavity widely so the soft tissues fall in and reduce its volume, and remove the tip cells or amputate the tip so the cavity has no dependent pocket. Remove the cholesteatoma matrix in continuity from the mastoid forward. Over a <b>fistula of the lateral canal</b>, leave the matrix until the end, then either leave it in place (exteriorised) or lift it under irrigation and cover the fistula at once with fascia or bone pate.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A shallow, smooth bowl with no overhanging edges and no cells left full of disease.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>The open labyrinth</h5><p>Suction on an open fistula, or peeling matrix off it carelessly, causes a dead ear. Never suction directly on it.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-5\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.5</span><h3>Reconstruct and graft</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Pack the middle ear with Gelfoam. Lay the dried fascia as an underlay graft, draped onto the tympanic facial nerve above and the facial ridge behind, to close off a shallow middle ear space, with ossicular reconstruction now or at a second stage. Pack lateral to the graft and drum remnant.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-6\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.6</span><h3>Meatoplasty</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Always perform a meatoplasty when the wall is taken down: the opening must let you see and clean the upper, posterior and lower parts of the bowl, and it will contract as it heals, so make it generous. Either extend the upper canal incision into the <b>incisura</b> and suture the conchal bowl back to the postauricular tissues with 3-0 absorbable sutures (too aggressive and the ear cups forward), or remove a <b>crescent of conchal cartilage</b> from behind, leaving the anterior skin, and suture the meatal skin back to the periosteum behind the cavity. Stent the meatus with rolled Telfa, an expanding wick or antibiotic gauze for 10 to 14 days.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A meatus that admits a fingertip and shows the whole cavity with the microscope.</li></ul></div></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c6\" data-t=\"Other operations\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">6</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter six</div><h2>Other operations</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">Bondy and radical.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.1</span><h3>Bondy modified radical mastoidectomy</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>For an attic cholesteatoma that has grown <b>lateral to the ossicles</b> with an intact pars tensa and middle ear, often in an only or better hearing ear: the disease is exteriorised through an endaural approach, the anterior buttress removed and the superior canal wall lowered to the level of the incus, without entering the middle ear. The matrix is left as the lining of the exteriorised attic.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.2</span><h3>Radical mastoidectomy</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>For irreversible middle ear disease that a canal wall down procedure cannot control. After a canal wall down mastoidectomy, the drum remnant, malleus and incus are removed (the stapes is kept), the mucosa is removed, the <b>Eustachian tube orifice is obliterated</b> with muscle, fat or fascia, and no reconstruction is attempted: the whole middle ear is exteriorised into the cavity.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.3</span><h3>Reconstruction and obliteration</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Rebuilding the canal wall gives the exposure of an open operation without a cavity, at the cost of foreign material in a contaminated field, and needs a second look. Obliterating the cavity makes long-term care easier but can bury matrix.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c7\" data-t=\"When it goes wrong\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">7</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter seven</div><h2>When it goes wrong</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">Drills at the drill.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.1</span><h3>Crisis drills</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arpath\"><h5>Bleeding from the sigmoid sinus or jugular bulb</h5><ol><li><span class=\"n\">1</span><div><b>Do not panic or pack blindly into the lumen</b><ul><li><span>take the bur away</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">2</span><div><b>Control</b><ul><li><span>a large piece of Surgicel or adrenaline-soaked Gelfoam over the hole with a flat pledget and gentle pressure</span></li><li><span>lower the head slightly if air embolism is a concern</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">3</span><div><b>Continue</b><ul><li><span>leave the patch in place and work elsewhere</span></li><li><span>bone wax for emissary vein bleeding</span></li></ul></div></li></ol></div><div class=\"arpath\"><h5>Dura exposed or torn, CSF in the field</h5><ol><li><span class=\"n\">1</span><div><b>Exposed dura</b><ul><li><span>healthy exposed dura under 1 cm needs nothing</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">2</span><div><b>Defect over 1 cm or any tear</b><ul><li><em>repair now</em><span>fascia under and over the defect, cartilage or bone for a large gap, tissue glue</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">3</span><div><b>Afterwards</b><ul><li><span>head up, no straining</span></li><li><span>watch for meningitis</span></li></ul></div></li></ol></div><div class=\"arpath\"><h5>Labyrinth opened (snail-track, endosteum or fluid seen)</h5><ol><li><span class=\"n\">1</span><div><b>Stop</b><ul><li><span>no suction on it</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">2</span><div><b>Seal</b><ul><li><span>cover at once with fascia, bone wax or bone pate</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">3</span><div><b>Afterwards</b><ul><li><span>steroids and antibiotics</span></li><li><span>test hearing and document</span></li></ul></div></li></ol></div><div class=\"arpath\"><h5>Facial weakness in recovery</h5><ol><li><span class=\"n\">1</span><div><b>Immediate, complete</b><ul><li><span>wait for local anaesthetic to wear off (a few hours)</span></li><li><span>if it persists and you did not positively see an intact nerve, re-explore early</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">2</span><div><b>Complete, but you are sure the nerve is intact</b><ul><li><span>steroids, eye care, electroneuronography after 3 days</span></li><li><span>explore if degeneration exceeds 90%</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">3</span><div><b>Integrity in doubt</b><ul><li><span>electromyography and CT</span></li><li><span>re-explore, decompress, or graft</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">4</span><div><b>Delayed or partial</b><ul><li><span>loosen the packing, steroids, eye care</span></li><li><span>recovery is the rule</span></li></ul></div></li></ol></div></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c8\" data-t=\"Aftercare\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">8</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter eight</div><h2>Aftercare</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">The cavity must heal dry.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.1</span><h3>After the operation</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>In recovery, check the <b>face</b>, a <b>tuning fork</b> (Weber should go to the operated ear) and for <b>nystagmus</b>. Most patients go home the same or next day with restricted activity, keeping the ear dry. Remove the meatal packing at 10 to 14 days and start antibiotic-steroid drops or rinses. An open cavity needs regular cleaning in clinic until it is fully epithelialised (often 6 to 12 weeks) and then at intervals for life. After an intact canal wall operation for cholesteatoma, plan a <b>second look</b> at 9 to 12 months or follow with diffusion-weighted MRI.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.2</span><h3>Complications</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Complications of mastoid surgery</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Complication</th><th scope=\"col\">Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bleeding, haematoma</td><td>Usually the sigmoid sinus, jugular bulb or emissary vein; pressure with Surgicel or Gelfoam</td></tr><tr><td>Facial paresis or palsy</td><td>Under 1% in primary and 4% to 10% in revision surgery; the second genu is the commonest site; mastoid surgery accounts for most otological facial nerve injuries</td></tr><tr><td>Conductive hearing loss</td><td>Ossicular or drum injury; reconstruct</td></tr><tr><td>Sensorineural loss, vertigo</td><td>Drill on the incus, stapes subluxation, fistula of the lateral canal or opened labyrinth</td></tr><tr><td>Dural injury, CSF leak, encephalocele</td><td>Repair defects over 1 cm and all tears</td></tr><tr><td>Altered taste</td><td>Chorda stretched or cut; often recovers in months</td></tr><tr><td>Wound infection, perichondritis</td><td>Antibiotics; drain a collection</td></tr><tr><td>Discharging cavity</td><td>High facial ridge, inadequate saucerisation, small meatus, residual cells, tubal reflux</td></tr><tr><td>Residual or recurrent cholesteatoma</td><td>More frequent after intact canal wall surgery; second look or MRI</td></tr><tr><td>Meatal stenosis, cupped ear</td><td>Too small or too aggressive a meatoplasty</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.3</span><h3>Alternatives</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>If surgery for cholesteatoma is deferred (frail patient, only hearing ear), regular microsuction, home irrigation and intermittent topical or systemic antibiotics may control the disease.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c9\" data-t=\"For the examination\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">9</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter nine</div><h2>For the examination</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">What the examiner wants to hear.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.1</span><h3>Where the books differ</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-differ\"><h5>Where the books differ</h5><div class=\"cols\"><div><b>Dhingra</b>describes three classical operations. Cortical (Schwartze) mastoidectomy exenterates the mastoid cells without disturbing the canal wall or middle ear, for acute coalescent mastoiditis and as an approach. Radical mastoidectomy makes the mastoid, middle ear, attic and antrum one cavity, removing the drum remnant, malleus and incus and closing the Eustachian tube. Modified radical mastoidectomy removes the disease and the canal wall but preserves as much of the drum, ossicles and hearing as possible. It gives the antrum's depth as about 12 to 15 mm beneath MacEwen's triangle, and stresses lowering the facial ridge, saucerisation and an adequate meatoplasty.</div><div><b>Lee</b>frames the choice as canal wall up versus canal wall down, with higher residual and recurrent disease after canal wall up surgery balanced against the lifelong cavity of the open technique.</div></div><p>Myers gives the landmark-by-landmark sequence, the facial recess, and the evidence on facial nerve injury and monitoring.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.2</span><h3>Viva essentials</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-exam\"><h5>What the examiner is looking for</h5><ul><li><b>MacEwen's triangle</b> and the depth of the antrum.</li><li>The landmarks in order: tegmen, sinus, Koerner's septum, antrum, <b>lateral canal</b>, incus, digastric ridge.</li><li>The <b>facial nerve</b> landmarks, and where it is injured (second genu).</li><li>Boundaries of the <b>facial recess</b>.</li><li>Canal wall up versus down: indications and trade-offs.</li><li>Why cavities discharge: high ridge, poor saucerisation, small meatus.</li><li>Management of the lateral canal fistula and postoperative facial palsy.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.3</span><h3>Common pitfalls</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-pit\"><h5>Common pitfalls</h5><ul><li>Drilling a deep, narrow hole instead of saucerising.</li><li>Drifting down and back in search of the antrum.</li><li>Mistaking Koerner's septum for the medial wall of the antrum.</li><li>Touching the incus with the bur.</li><li>A small bur, or a cutting bur, deep at the tip; a diamond bur without irrigation over the nerve.</li><li>A high facial ridge and an inadequate meatoplasty.</li><li>Using muscle relaxant with the nerve monitor on.</li></ul></div></div></details></section>", "stand": "Mastoidectomy is sculpting in bone: you remove the air cells of the mastoid in a fixed order, finding one landmark before you look for the next, with the dura above, the sigmoid sinus behind, the labyrinth in the depth and the facial nerve running through the middle of the field. It is the operation for cholesteatoma and complicated mastoiditis, and the doorway to cochlear implantation, facial nerve and skull base surgery. This chapter covers the choice between keeping the canal wall and taking it down, the landmarks in the order you meet them, each stage of the intact canal wall operation, the facial recess, the canal wall down cavity and meatoplasty, and what to do when the sinus, dura, labyrinth or nerve is injured.", "ch": ["Operating note", "Anatomy you must see", "The operation, stage by stage", "The operation: facial recess (posterior tympanotomy)", "The operation: canal wall down", "Other operations", "When it goes wrong", "Aftercare", "For the examination"], "w": 4166, "n": 32, "st": [{"c": "stage by stage", "t": "Set up, inject and incise", "d": "Prepare the field, check the monitor, and examine and clean the ear under the microscope to confirm the findings. If a tympanomeatal flap will be raised, inject lidocaine with adrenaline in four quadrants at the bony-cartilaginous junction (the medial limit of the hair-bearing skin), bevel to the bone and subperiosteal, until the skin blanches down to the drum; then make canal incisions at 12 and 6 o'clock joined by a vertical cut halfway between the annulus and the junction, forming a laterally based vascular strip and a medially based tympanomeatal flap. Make the postauricular incision a few millimetres behind the crease, from above the root of the helix to the tip, down to the loose areolar tissue over the temporalis fascia. In children, keep the lower limb further back and off the tip.", "f": [1], "see": "Canal skin blanched to the drum, and the glistening temporalis fascia with loose areolar tissue over it.", "risk": "The mastoid tip is undeveloped in small children and the nerve leaves the skull superficially. Curve the incision back and stop short of the tip.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s3-1"}, {"c": "stage by stage", "t": "Expose the cortex and the landmarks", "d": "Incise the periosteum along the linea temporalis with a vertical limb to the tip (a \"T\" or \"7\"), elevate it forward to expose the posterior bony canal, lift the vascular strip, and retract the pinna forward with a tape or Penrose passed through the canal. Harvest temporalis fascia now and set it to dry. Identify the linea temporalis, the spine of Henle and the cribriform area.", "f": [], "see": "The whole mastoid cortex from the root of the zygoma to the tip, the posterior canal wall, and MacEwen's triangle with its small pits.", "risk": "", "pearl": "Exposure decides the operation. If you cannot see the zygomatic root and the tip, you will drill a deep narrow hole, which is how facial nerves are injured.", "id": "a-s3-2"}, {"c": "stage by stage", "t": "Make the first cuts and saucerise", "d": "With the largest cutting bur that fits and continuous suction irrigation, make the first cut along the linea temporalis, the second parallel to the posterior canal wall, and join them behind along the line of the sigmoid sinus, forming a triangle. Remove the cortex and the air cells within it in long strokes parallel to the structure you are approaching, bevelling (saucerising) the edges widely so light and instruments reach the depth.", "f": [2], "see": "A wide, shallow, kidney-shaped cavity with sloping edges, deepest at its front and top.", "risk": "Keep the deepest point over the antrum, behind the spine of Henle. Beginners drift down and back, away from the antrum and into the plane of the facial nerve and the sigmoid sinus. If you cannot find the antrum, go forward and up, never down.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s3-3"}, {"c": "stage by stage", "t": "Define the tegmen and the sinus", "d": "Thin the bone above until the tegmen shows as a pink plate, and behind until the sigmoid sinus shows blue through its plate, leaving a thin shell on each. Open the sinodural angle between them. These two plates are the upper and posterior walls of your cavity and are followed forward to the antrum.", "f": [], "see": "A smooth pink tegmen above, a blue sinus plate behind, and the posterior canal wall thinned in front.", "risk": "A cutting bur run across exposed dura or the sinus tears it. Change to a diamond bur as you approach them, and stroke along them, not into them.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s3-4"}, {"c": "stage by stage", "t": "Enter the antrum", "d": "Using the tegmen as the roof and the canal wall as the front, remove Koerner's septum and enter the antrum. Confirm it by its size and by what lies in its floor: the dense, pale dome of the lateral semicircular canal.", "f": [], "see": "A large air space or sac of disease, and in its floor the smooth ivory bulge of the lateral canal.", "risk": "", "pearl": "If you think you are in the antrum but cannot see the lateral canal, you are probably still lateral to Koerner's septum. The antrum is deeper than you expect.", "id": "a-s3-5"}, {"c": "stage by stage", "t": "Open the aditus and find the incus", "d": "Change to a smaller bur and drill forward toward the root of the zygoma, between the tegmen and the canal wall, keeping the bur turning away from the ossicles, until the short process of the incus appears in the fossa incudis. Flood the cavity: the incus is often first seen as a refraction under the water. Remove remaining cells from the tip, exposing the digastric ridge, and from the sinodural angle, as the disease requires.", "f": [3], "see": "The incus in its fossa, the lateral canal behind and below it, the digastric ridge at the tip, and an intact thin posterior canal wall.", "risk": "A rotating bur touching the incus transmits its energy to the inner ear and causes high-tone sensorineural loss. Leave a sliver of bone over the short process and remove it with a pick or curette.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s3-6"}, {"c": "stage by stage", "t": "Close", "d": "Replace the vascular strip and pack the canal with Gelfoam or Telfa. Close the periosteum (which stops the scar retracting into the cavity), then the deep dermis with 3-0 or 4-0 absorbable sutures and the skin with a fine absorbable suture. Apply a mastoid dressing for 24 hours. Count the pledgets.", "f": [], "see": "", "risk": "", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s3-7"}, {"c": "facial recess (posterior tympanotomy)", "t": "Thin the canal wall and set the landmarks", "d": "The recess is bounded by the incus buttress above, the facial nerve medially and behind, and the chorda tympani laterally and in front. Before entering it, have the lateral canal, the fossa incudis and the digastric ridge in view, and thin the posterior canal wall well: a thick wall hides the recess and forces you medially toward the nerve.", "f": [], "see": "The short process of the incus pointing to the recess, and a canal wall thin enough to see instruments through.", "risk": "", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s4-1"}, {"c": "facial recess (posterior tympanotomy)", "t": "Find the nerve and open the recess", "d": "With a small diamond bur and copious irrigation, drill just below the incus buttress in strokes parallel to the course of the facial nerve, never across it. Identify the nerve through a thin covering of bone as a pink-white cord with its fine vessels, and the chorda coming off it laterally. Enter the middle ear at the upper end of the recess, where it is widest.", "f": [], "see": "The facial nerve seen through bone medially, the chorda laterally, and a dark slit between them opening into the middle ear.", "risk": "A diamond bur generates heat quickly; without irrigation it burns the nerve through intact bone. Keep the water running and leave a shell of bone on the nerve.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s4-2"}, {"c": "facial recess (posterior tympanotomy)", "t": "Widen and inspect", "d": "Widen the opening by removing bone medial to the chorda. Through it you see the incudostapedial joint, the stapes and stapedius tendon, the promontory and the round window niche. In an extended facial recess, the chorda is sacrificed and bone removed down to the inferior annulus to expose the hypotympanum and the medial surface of the drum.", "f": [4], "see": "The incudostapedial joint and round window niche, with the sinus tympani hidden medial to the nerve (use an angled endoscope for it).", "risk": "", "pearl": "Dissect disease off the stapes from back to front, in the direction of the stapedius tendon, which braces the stapes against your pull.", "id": "a-s4-3"}, {"c": "canal wall down", "t": "Decide and deal with the incus", "d": "Complete the cortical mastoidectomy first. If the incus is diseased or the chain must be interrupted, separate the incudostapedial joint before taking the wall down, so that drilling on the bridge cannot be transmitted to the stapes. If the incus can stay, leave a thin bridge of bone over its short process and remove it with a pick or curette.", "f": [], "see": "A divided incudostapedial joint, or a protected incus under a thin bony bridge.", "risk": "", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-1"}, {"c": "canal wall down", "t": "Take down the wall", "d": "Remove the posterior canal wall with a 4 mm cutting bur. Think of it as three parts: the anterior buttress (at the tympanosquamous suture and the lateral attic wall), the posterior buttress (which becomes the facial ridge), and the bridge between them over the aditus. Remove the bridge, then lower both buttresses so the attic roof runs smoothly into the tegmen and the anterior attic is open.", "f": [5], "see": "The attic, antrum and mastoid as one cavity with the canal, with no overhang at the anterior attic.", "risk": "", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-2"}, {"c": "canal wall down", "t": "Lower the facial ridge", "d": "Thin the bone over the second genu and vertical segment with a diamond bur, in strokes parallel to the nerve, until the ridge is level with the floor of the canal and the nerve is covered by only a thin plate. A high facial ridge leaves a sump behind it that collects debris and discharges for life.", "f": [], "see": "A low, smooth ridge with the nerve's course visible through bone, the lateral canal dome above it, and a round rather than kidney-shaped cavity.", "risk": "This is where the nerve is injured: just below the lateral canal, at the level of the fossa incudis. Above the canal you are safe; at and below it, use the diamond bur and the monitor.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-3"}, {"c": "canal wall down", "t": "Saucerise and remove the disease", "d": "Bevel the upper and posterior edges of the cavity widely so the soft tissues fall in and reduce its volume, and remove the tip cells or amputate the tip so the cavity has no dependent pocket. Remove the cholesteatoma matrix in continuity from the mastoid forward. Over a fistula of the lateral canal, leave the matrix until the end, then either leave it in place (exteriorised) or lift it under irrigation and cover the fistula at once with fascia or bone pate.", "f": [], "see": "A shallow, smooth bowl with no overhanging edges and no cells left full of disease.", "risk": "Suction on an open fistula, or peeling matrix off it carelessly, causes a dead ear. Never suction directly on it.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-4"}, {"c": "canal wall down", "t": "Reconstruct and graft", "d": "Pack the middle ear with Gelfoam. Lay the dried fascia as an underlay graft, draped onto the tympanic facial nerve above and the facial ridge behind, to close off a shallow middle ear space, with ossicular reconstruction now or at a second stage. Pack lateral to the graft and drum remnant.", "f": [], "see": "", "risk": "", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-5"}, {"c": "canal wall down", "t": "Meatoplasty", "d": "Always perform a meatoplasty when the wall is taken down: the opening must let you see and clean the upper, posterior and lower parts of the bowl, and it will contract as it heals, so make it generous. Either extend the upper canal incision into the incisura and suture the conchal bowl back to the postauricular tissues with 3-0 absorbable sutures (too aggressive and the ear cups forward), or remove a crescent of conchal cartilage from behind, leaving the anterior skin, and suture the meatal skin back to the periosteum behind the cavity. Stent the meatus with rolled Telfa, an expanding wick or antibiotic gauze for 10 to 14 days.", "f": [], "see": "A meatus that admits a fingertip and shows the whole cavity with the microscope.", "risk": "", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-6"}]};
